    "lawCode": "Add §33.06, amd §45.07, Ment Hyg L",
    "lawSection": "Mental Hygiene Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6772A\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to the\nestablishment of procedures or mechanisms to receive allegations of\nabuse or mistreatment of individuals served by agencies and providers\nlicensed or operated under the mental hygiene law\n\n\n PURPOSE :\nTo establish toll free numbers for the reporting of abuse of persons\nwith disabilities being served by state licensed or operated\nfacilities.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\nSection one of the bill adds a new section 33.06 entitled \"Reports of\nAbuse or Mistreatment\" to the Mental Hygiene Law. Section two of the\nbill amends paragraph one of subdivision (c) of section 45.07 of the\nmental hygiene law, as amended by Chapter 323 of the Laws of 2008, as\ndescribed in the bill.  Section three of the bill provides for an\neffective date of 180 days after enactment.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nLast year, both houses of our legislature unanimously approved\nlegislation (A.8972A/S.5930-A) creating a toll free hotline to report\nthe abuse or mistreatment of adults with developmental and or mental\ndisabilities who are served by state licensed and state operated\nresidential care facilities. Citing fiscal concerns and duplication of\nsome existing hotlines, the Governor vetoed the bill last year.\n\nSince that time, a series of meetings have taken place with the\nGovernor's office and state agencies. This new bill contains parts of\ncompromise language that will enable the Commissioners of the relevant\nagencies to establish procedures for a hotline. While some of the\nstate agencies currently operate toll-free hotlines, their existence\nis not in law and not statutorily required. This bill would remedy\nthat situation.\n\nSome of our state's mentally disabled adults have cognitive\nfunctioning that is less than a non-disabled child. This makes them\nvulnerable to abuse in situations where they are unable to speak for\nthemselves or to successfully relate an incident. The creation of a\nhotline will better ensure the reporting of abuse and suspected abuse\nof these vulnerable individuals, who are adults by law, but lack many\nof the adult skills necessary to advocate for themselves in an abusive\nsituation.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2009 - S.5930 Vetoed\n44\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nMinimal, but undetermined.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nEffective on the one hundred eightieth day after it shall have become\na law.\n",
